orth  all came from france and so germans  the  sort of highly educated germans  spoke french  which is not the case now today they  speak english i mean in sweden german  was the more dominant second language  had a head of  of english earlier in the previous  century so things can change  and if you look around the world  uh i mean in europe there's 100 million  people who speak german  and german is very easy for dutch people  and scandinavians to learn  and germany is a major economic force  it's germany itself and you add in  austria and the german-speaking part of  switzerland that's a dominant economy  uh  and and as you move eastward uh you know  on the continent you get into  of course the countries of eastern  europe which  now of course they're very concerned  about establishing their independence  from russia because it just got out from  under the  the control either as a satellite  country or as a part of the former  soviet union  so the baltic states and poland and the  czech republic and so forth they want to  affirm their independence and learning  english instead of russian is a way of  doing that  however they all speak nicely they'll  speak but russian is quite common  especially in in the countries of the  former soviet union  and when i listened to echamas v and  they interviewed people from georgia  from moldavia from kazakhstan  from latvia i'm just so impressed with  the level of the russia they speak  flawlessly i mean these are politicians  in those countries  so  russian covers a large geographical area  so it whereas it used to be primarily  the language of the soviet union it's  now an international language i mean  turkish  in central asia  i have no idea how mutually  comprehensible uh uyghur and anatolian  turkish is  but between you know uzbekistan and  azerbaijan and turkmenistan and so forth  there's a lot of countries in there and  a lot of geographic space where turkish  is spoken  not to mention arabic which covers north  africa and a good part of the middle  east  hindi which is a common  common language  that's fairly well understood i gather  because i don't speak it uh  in the  south uh in south asia  japanese korean chinese are all quite  different  but at least the japanese and the  koreans have a head start in learning  chinese because they  may not still be the case in korea but  certainly the japanese they have the  chinese characters  uh latin america spanish  the uh  the  sort of latin mediterranean italy france  spain speak essentially the same  language it's not very difficult for  them to to learn the other language so i  mean all of those people today are  happier speaking english that's because  that's the fashion because that's  perceived to be more international and  perhaps there's rivalry maybe the  italian  doesn't want to speak french he'd rather  speak english because  mutually they're trying to be more  arrogant than the other i mean there's  all kinds of reasons but it's all  attitude  so if the attitude changes  if the attitude changes  uh i mean i i  i don't see any reason why we couldn't  have four or five international  languages and part of that is the  recognition that it's not a big deal if  you can speak one second language you  can speak two or three or four  i believe as long as the  teaching methods  and as long as our attitude towards it  changes and  historically  there have been many societies and many  sort of  social classes where it was not at all  unusual for people to speak  three or four languages so my hope is  that we are returning to an era of  multilingualism  and i think that the present crisis and  the people's attitudes towards the  dominant role that the english-speaking  world has played  in in in the world economy and in the  development of of of world affairs and  even in academic affairs that it might  they might suffer english is going to  suffer a loss of prestige  and that might give a chance to other  languages to be considered 